{"title":"1965 AHSME Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_9\">Problem 9</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 9\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The vertex of the parabola <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/4/a3471cc2d12ec07d0ed2a13dd20f041dc1cc31ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = x^2 - 8x + c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"20\" /> will be a point on the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis if the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/5/d6591f14ed8f572ffd9c7e2f38d76214565224b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ - 16 \\qquad  \\textbf{(B) }\\ - 4 \\qquad  \\textbf{(C) }\\ 4 \\qquad  \\textbf{(D) }\\ 8 \\qquad  \\textbf{(E) }\\ 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"436\" height=\"17\" />  \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that if the vertex of a parabola is on the x-axis, then the x-coordinate of the vertex must be a solution to the quadratic. Since the quadratic is strictly increasing on either side of the vertex, the solution must have double multiplicity, or the quadratic is a perfect square trinomial. This means that for the vertex of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/4/a3471cc2d12ec07d0ed2a13dd20f041dc1cc31ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = x^2 - 8x + c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"20\" /> to be on the x-axis, the trinomial must be a perfect square, and have discriminant of zero. So,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/0/2e013d4048fb5ac3a27e149a9295ac505999a86f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}     0 &amp;= b^2-4ac\\\\     0 &amp;= (-8)^2-4c\\\\     c &amp;=  64\\\\     c &amp;= 16\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"116\" height=\"93\" />\n</p><p>Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/1/4514788b72d9f2c0475055a174f7471c1e7cf8ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=16$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />, and our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/f/6bfc0829f3f29bbf35d0a861644e00cfef0f50ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"39\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME\" title=\"1965 AHSME\">1965 AHSC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1965 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1965 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"exte